STAGE 1 -- "the F lane alternates over 8 consecutive frames"
  A wrong bit alignment puts this test on a payload lane.  A lane that
  passes is a lane that holds stage 1 open.

  payload lane              P(bit flips)  P(8 in a row)  windows passing
  CVSD speech                      0.957          0.706            0.699
  CVSD silence                     1.000          1.000            1.000
  CVSD, -40 dB tone                1.000          0.999            0.999
  data, all ones                   0.000          0.000            0.000
  data, octet 10110010             0.750          0.100            0.000
  random data                      0.500          0.004            0.004

  CVSD silence is exactly 1010..., so it alternates by construction.
  spec.md 3.3: an alternating header is "imitated by CVSD silence" --
  7/8 and 15/16 skews false-locked, permanently.  Stage 1 is that header.

SUPERFRAME PHASE -- all 64 hook-state combinations, all 8 local offsets
  Bit alignment is correct here.  Only the superframe phase is in question,
  and the two pads are the only thing that pins it.

  hook combinations admitting >= 1 wrong superframe phase: 56 / 64  (87.5%)
  wrong phases admitted, mean 1.50, max 3
  offsets never admitted: [4]

  Why: the pads sit 4 apart in an 8-state superframe, so the signature
  only ever compares channels 4 apart -- (1,4), (2,5), (3,6).  One wrong
  phase is admitted per pair whose two channels differ in hook state:
    ch4 off-hook, ch1 on-hook -> offset 1 holds;  reversed -> offset 5 holds
    ch5 off-hook, ch2 on-hook -> offset 2 holds;  reversed -> offset 6 holds
    ch6 off-hook, ch3 on-hook -> offset 3 holds;  reversed -> offset 7 holds

  Worked example, ch4 on a call and ch1 idle (a1=0, a4=1):
    hook states (0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0) -> offsets [1] pass the signature forever.
    At offset 1 the receiver is bit- and frame-aligned, audio is bit-exact,
    and every hook state is delivered to the wrong channel latch.
    Nothing in mfspec 5 can detect it.

