LINE ERRORS -- what one bad F bit costs

  mfspec 5 gives no tracking rule and no bad-frame counter, so a single
  F-bit error is a lost lock and a swallowed bit.  Mean time between
  F-bit errors, at a 64 kHz frame rate:

       BER   F errors/s   mean time between
     1e-04          6.4           156.25 ms
     1e-03         64.0            15.62 ms
     1e-02        640.0             1.56 ms
     3e-02       1920.0             0.52 ms

  spec.md 7.3 carries a +1/-1 trip-16 counter for exactly this reason,
  and measures 0 false reframes to 3e-2 (0-1 at 3e-2, 8 at 5e-2).

  mfspec receiver at 1e-3, true skew: first lock at bit 444, sf offset 0

ENCODER -- gate-level model against the frame builder
  4096 frames, random payload and hook states: bit-exact

  The model needs, and only needs:
    3-stage frame counter          b0..b2, addresses the frame mux directly
    3-stage superframe counter     addresses the signalling mux; its LSB IS F
    8:1 frame mux                  in0 = F, in1 = signalling mux, in2..7 = C1..C6
    8:1 signalling mux             in3 tied high, in7 tied low, rest = A1..A6

  No S/T select gate, no framing-data AND, no mux-output override, and no
  multiframe pattern generator.  The 16-bit frame needs all four, plus a
  second 8:1 mux and a selector to reach 14 payload positions
  (seven-channel.md 3.2: "one extra package on the transmitter, possibly two").

  Placing the pads at states 3 and 7 does pay off as claimed: both are
  b1.b0 = 1, and the expected value is the complement of b2, so the RX
  guard is one AND for the enable and one XOR for the compare.

BIT BUDGET at 512 kbps, both formats
                                16-bit, N=7  8-bit, mfspec
  frame length, bits                     16              8
  frame rate, kHz                        32             64
  voice channels                          7              6
  per-channel rate, kbps                 32             64
  voice payload, kbps                   224            384
  signalling, kbps                      224             48
  framing, kbps                          64             80
  voice, % of line                    43.8%          75.0%
  signalling per channel            32 kbps         8 kbps

  Same line, same code, same baud: 1.71x the voice payload, because the
  16-bit frame spends 43.75% of the line sending hook states 32000 times a
  second.  spec.md 9 needs one bit per 2 ms.

CONSTANT PAYLOAD -- false locks over all 64 hook states x all 8 skews
  (bit level, no line impairment; 0.1 s = 6400 frames per run)

  payload                       false locks  no lock at all
  all six lanes constant 0          0 / 512         0 / 512
  all six lanes constant 1          0 / 512         0 / 512
  lanes alternate 0 / 1             0 / 512         0 / 512
  one constant 0 in silence         0 / 512         0 / 512
  one constant 1 in silence         0 / 512         0 / 512
  one constant 0 in speech          0 / 512         0 / 512
  two adjacent constants 0,1        0 / 512         0 / 512

  Why constant payload is SAFE for this header, both ways round:
    F position: a constant lane never alternates -> stage 1 fails at once.
    S position: a constant lane cannot be both 1 at state 3 and 0 at
                state 7 -> the guard fails deterministically.
    The S lane itself cannot alternate for 8 frames either: an
    alternating run needs index 3 = 1 and index 7 = 0, and both
    01010101 and 10101010 fail one of them.  So skew 1 never holds.

  TRACKING at the true alignment (mfspec 5 re-verified every superframe,
  strictest reading: any pad mismatch unlocks):

  payload                       locked frames  unlocks  off-phase
  all six lanes constant 0               6385        0          0
  all six lanes constant 1               6385        0          0
  lanes alternate 0 / 1                  6385        0          0
  one constant 0 in silence              6385        0          0
  one constant 1 in silence              6385        0          0
  one constant 0 in speech               6385        0          0
  two adjacent constants 0,1             6385        0          0

  Lock is held on every constant payload: F and S are dedicated bits,
  so the header is independent of what the channels carry.  This is a
  real advantage over the original static (0,1) header, which a constant
  data slot false-locks permanently (spec.md 3.3, 40/192 measured).

