RANDOM DATA ON ALL SIX CHANNELS
  2000000 frames = 31.25 s of line time, 16000000 line bits.
  Unconstrained random data is the BENIGN end of the payload space:
  a random lane alternates with p=0.5, so it passes stage 1 on only
  0.4% of windows (4.1).  It is octet-PERIODIC data that is fatal (4.2).

  Long run on a clean line, both readings of mfspec 5:

  reading                             locked frames  unlocks  off-phase
  'strict': any pad mismatch unlocks        1999985        0          0
  'none': never re-checked after lock        1999985        0          0

  Lock is held for the full 31 s with zero unlocks: F and S are
  dedicated bits, so random payload cannot disturb a correct lock,
  exactly as constant payload cannot (5).

  Long run with line errors, strict reading:

   line BER  locked frames  unlocks  off-phase  off-phase %  mean resync
      0e+00        1999985        0          0       0.000%      15.0 fr
      1e-05        1997176       26          0       0.000%     108.6 fr
      1e-04        1974060      246          0       0.000%     105.4 fr
      1e-03        1760818     2238          3       0.000%     106.9 fr
      1e-02         785092    10011          2       0.000%     121.4 fr

  Every unlock costs a full re-acquisition, and mfspec has no
  hysteresis to absorb one bad pad sample.  spec.md 7.3 measures 0
  false reframes to 3e-2 on the same class of line.

  RESYNC after an induced slip, every slip size, clean line:

   slip   strict: bad / relock       none: bad frames
      1                  1 / 1                 999999
      2                  0 / 0                 999999
      3                  1 / 1                 999999
      4                  0 / 0                 999999
      5                  0 / 0                 999999
      6                  0 / 0                 999999
      7                  0 / 0                 999999

  bad = frames emitted at a wrong phase after the slip;
  relock = frames until the first correctly-phased frame.
  Under 'none' a slip is NEVER detected and the channels stay rotated
  for the rest of the run -- which is spec.md 7.4's point restated:
  a misaligned frame fails only part of the time, so a rule that needs
  a run of bad frames does not fire.  mfspec has neither rule.

  Where a resync can LAND: false locks over many hunts on random data
  (8 skews x 250 payload seeds, 2000 frames each):

  false locks: 0 / 2000  (0.00% of hunts)
  acquisition, true phase: 15-40 frames, mean 22.5 = 0.352 ms

  Confirmation strength.  Analytically, ONE wrong phase tested once
  against random data passes with probability
    stage 1, 8 alternating frames   0.5^8 = 3.9e-03
    stage 2, two pad bits           0.25
    product                               = 9.8e-04  ~ 2^-10
  against the 16-bit framer's 32 good frames at <=50% each = 2^-32.
  The MEASURED per-hunt rate is lower than that product (0 of 2000,
  so <=1.8e-03 at 95%), because a failing phase is abandoned after one
  bad frame rather than retried -- the hunt walks away before it can
  accumulate an 8-frame run.  So against random data the weak
  confirmation does not bite in practice; 4.2's periodic-data hole is
  structural rather than statistical, and no confirmation count
  reaches it.  What mfspec buys for the weaker check is speed: 15-40
  frames at 64 kHz against 31-49 at 32 kHz, so about 3x faster in time.

