      0e+00         999968           0          0
      1e-04         999968           0          0
      1e-03         999968           0          0
      1e-02         999959           0          0

BIT COUNT UNDER IMPULSE -- the mechanism behind 8.1
  A decoder that emits a different NUMBER of bits than were sent has
  slipped: the frame re-phases and must reacquire.  Emitted minus sent,
  over 4 seeds, 128000 line bits:

   impulse/s                    biphase              ami
           0           [-1, -1, -1, -1]     [0, 0, 0, 0]
          10        [-15, -90, -86, -1]     [0, 0, 0, 0]
         100   [-243, -406, -485, -200]     [0, 0, 0, 0]
        1000 [-3309, -3364, -3662, -2966]    [0, 0, -1, 0]

  The monostable decides on transition TIMING and has no independent
  time reference, so a transient that adds or swallows an edge changes
  the bit count.  AMI is clocked: it emits exactly one bit per recovered
  clock period whatever the line does, so the count cannot drift and the
  +1/-1 counter only ever sees ordinary bit errors.  The -1 at zero
  impulses is a constant end effect, not a slip.

