ZERO RUNS -- the frame bounds them, so AMI needs no zero substitution
  Worst run of zeros on the line, over all 64 hook states:

  all constant 0      15
  all constant 1       2
  CVSD silence         2
  CVSD speech          8
  octet 10110010       9
  random data         13

  worst over everything: 15 bits.
  F alternates, so a mark lands every 2 frames = 16 line bits whatever
  the payload does.  15 is STRUCTURAL, not statistical, and it is the
  8-bit frame that makes it so.  B8ZS substitutes at 8 zeros and HDB3
  at 4, so neither is needed -- the framing bit already does the job.
  T = 1110 on F (10, item 1) keeps the same bound: F is 0 one frame in
  four, so the longest gap is still 2 frames.


OPTION A -- analog tank (rectify, resonator, flywheel)
  Sampling phase trimmed once at nominal: phase 0, BER 0.0e+00.
  BER on the worst payload (constant 0), tank detuned:

       Q  amp left    0.00%    0.10%    0.25%    0.50%    1.00%    2.00%
      20      0.09 9.2e-02 9.2e-02 9.2e-02 9.0e-02 9.0e-02 6.2e-02
      50      0.39 1.2e-01 3.1e-02 4.7e-02 4.7e-02 4.7e-02 6.2e-02
     100      0.62      ok      ok      ok 3.1e-02 4.7e-02 6.2e-02
     200      0.79      ok      ok      ok 3.1e-02 4.7e-02 1.2e-01
     500      0.91      ok      ok 1.6e-02 4.7e-02 4.7e-02 1.2e-01

  Two effects, and the second is the binding one:
    coasting decay over 15 idle cycles = exp(-pi*15/Q)  -> wants Q high
    static phase shift when detuned    = atan(2*Q*df/f) -> wants Q low
  The measured edge sits at df/f ~ 1/(2Q): ok at 0.25% with Q=100,
  broken at 0.5%.  So a tank wants Q ~ 100 AND tuning inside ~0.25%.
  A 1% L and a 1% C give about 1% -- an LC tank does not reach it.
  That is a ceramic resonator or a crystal, per line rate, on the
  receiver's rate plug (line-receiver.md 8).


OPTION B -- oversampling DPLL off the local 4.096 MHz crystal
  The RX sampling clock is the local crystal; the far end's is an
  independent one, so the offset is what two crystals disagree by.

  4.096 MHz divides every family rate exactly:
    1024 kbps -> 4x,  512 kbps -> 8x,  256 kbps -> 16x,  128 kbps -> 32x,  64 kbps -> 64x

    TX ppm    const 0     random   clean line
         0         ok         ok
       100         ok         ok
       200         ok         ok
       500         ok         ok
      1000         ok         ok
      5000         ok         ok

    TX ppm    sd    const 0     random   with noise
         0   0.3         ok         ok
         0   0.4         ok    3.1e-05
         0   0.5         ok    6.9e-04
       100   0.3         ok         ok
       100   0.4         ok         ok
       100   0.5    1.0e-05    5.2e-04

  Two crystals at +/-100 ppm disagree by at most 200 ppm = 0.02%,
  a phase slip of 0.0030 bit across a 15-zero gap.  Against the
  tank's 0.25% requirement that is 12x margin on frequency, and the
  coast is nothing.  Error-free to 1000 ppm on the worst payload.

  The cost is architectural, not electrical: spec.md 6.1's receiver
  has NO clock on the timing path, and this gives it one.  That is the
  property being traded, and 6.3 already prices it -- the oversampling
  DPLL is the reference receiver there.

IMPULSE SUSCEPTIBILITY -- differential Manchester (== "biphase" here)
  Mechanism: the rings are 15-80 kHz against a 512 kHz symbol rate, so
  each one acts as a slowly varying OFFSET.  With the signal at +/-1 and
  hysteresis h, an offset A sticks the comparator when A > 1-h: one
  polarity stops crossing, every transition in the ring is lost, and the
  decoder loses that many bits.  Ring length 20-200 us = 10-100 bits.

  Bits lost vs impulse amplitude, fixed rate 200/s, 4 seeds:

   amplitude  predicted                  bits lost
        0.20          -               [1, 1, 1, 1]
        0.50          -               [1, 2, 1, 1]
        0.80          -             [9, 16, 13, 2]
        0.95          -           [31, 39, 50, 25]
        1.10     sticks       [250, 278, 301, 206]
        1.50     sticks   [1665, 1820, 1966, 1423]
        3.00     sticks   [6413, 7050, 7657, 5694]

  The threshold sits where predicted, just below 1.0x the signal peak.
  An impulse smaller than the signal cannot blank a polarity and costs
  nothing; one larger than it costs the whole ring.

  Hysteresis moves that threshold the WRONG way (A > 1-h), fixed
  amplitude 0.9, rate 200/s -- so the impulse-optimal comparator is
  a tight one, which is the opposite of what Gaussian noise wants:

  hysteresis  threshold                  bits lost
        0.02       0.98                   [13, 23]
        0.05       0.95                   [14, 25]
        0.10       0.90                   [22, 30]
        0.20       0.80                   [77, 85]


LINK QUALITY -- recovered voice through the full chain, 1.0 s clip
  segSNR of channel 1 against the SAME codec with no link in the path,
  so the figure is what the carrier adds (seven-channel.md 4).
  Receiver is the spec.md 7 framer on the 16-bit frame -- a properly
  built one -- so this isolates the LINE CODE, not mfspec's missing
  loss-of-frame counter.

  CVSD alone, no link, whole clip: 8.17 dB segSNR.
  "vs codec" below compares against the codec over the SAME window,
  so it is the carrier's contribution and not which speech landed in
