POLARITY -- is AMI blind to a tip/ring swap, as biphase is?

  EXACT TEST: the complete received waveform is inverted, impairments
  included, which is what swapping the two conductors actually does.

  condition              code      identical  bits differing
  clean                  biphase         YES               0
  clean                  ami             YES               0
  noise sd 0.4           biphase         YES               0
  noise sd 0.4           ami             YES               0
  noise sd 0.6           biphase         YES               0
  noise sd 0.6           ami             YES               0
  impulse 100/s          biphase         YES               0
  impulse 100/s          ami             YES               0
  attenuated 0.25x       biphase         YES               0
  attenuated 0.25x       ami             YES               0

  All conditions bit-identical: True.
  Both codes are exactly polarity-blind, including while the link is
  making errors -- the errors themselves are the same errors.

  ENSEMBLE TEST: signal inverted, receiver-referred noise added after,
  so the two runs are genuinely different experiments.  Individual
  errors MUST differ; the error RATE must not.  Mean BER over 6 seeds:

  condition              code         normal   inverted
  noise sd 0.4           biphase     0.0e+00    0.0e+00
  noise sd 0.4           ami         1.3e-05    5.2e-06
  noise sd 0.6           biphase     5.2e-06    2.6e-06
  noise sd 0.6           ami         3.4e-03    3.4e-03
  impulse 100/s          biphase     4.4e-01    4.4e-01
  impulse 100/s          ami         5.9e-03    6.0e-03
  attenuated 0.25x       biphase     0.0e+00    0.0e+00
  attenuated 0.25x       ami         1.3e-05    5.2e-06

  The rates match; only which bits happened to break differs.  An
  earlier version of this test compared those two columns bit for bit
  and appeared to show AMI failing -- it was comparing two different
  noise experiments, not two polarities.

  END TO END, 16-bit frame, spec.md 7 framer, clean line:

  code       polarity  locks  lanes   LOF
  biphase      normal    yes     ok     0
  biphase     swapped    yes     ok     0
  ami          normal    yes     ok     0
  ami         swapped    yes     ok     0

  Why AMI is blind, and why it costs nothing:
    The decision is |x| > thresh*peak, so the sign never enters
    (phy.ami_decode; c_ami_decode uses fabs).
    A full-wave front end is not an extra part bolted on for this --
    AMI NEEDS one anyway, because marks arrive as both + and - pulses
    and a half-wave receiver would miss every other one.  Polarity
    neutrality comes free with the receiver you must build regardless.
    The alternation rule is symmetric too: inverting +,-,+,- gives
    -,+,-,+, still valid, and a bipolar violation stays a violation
    under either polarity, so even BPV error detection survives.
  Biphase is blind for a different reason: the decision is same-vs-
  different half cells and inversion flips both (spec.md 5).

WHY AMI NEEDS A TIME REFERENCE AND BIPHASE DOES NOT
  It is the DYNAMIC RANGE of the timing measurement, not the presence
  of timing information:

    biphase  intervals are 0.5 or 1.0 cell   -> 2:1, and spec.md 6.2
             measures +/-13% tolerance as sufficient
    AMI      gaps run 1 to 16 bit periods    -> 16:1, and a gap of k
             mis-rounds once tol*k > 0.5, so 16 needs tol < 3.1%
  Same information, four times the precision, purely because the frame
  lets zeros run 15 long (9.1).

OPTION A -- no clock at all: quantise the gap between marks

   RC tol  random BER   count |  const 0 BER    count
     0.0%     0.0e+00      -8 |      0.0e+00      -15
     0.5%     0.0e+00      -8 |      0.0e+00      -15
     1.0%     0.0e+00      -8 |      0.0e+00      -15
     2.0%     0.0e+00      -8 |      0.0e+00      -15
     3.0%     6.5e-02      -9 |      1.5e-01     -765
     5.0%     4.8e-01    -136 |      1.4e-01    -3014
    10.0%     4.9e-01   -1566 |      1.4e-01    -3014

  ("count" is emitted minus sent; the -8 / -15 at small tol is the
  stream starting at the first mark and ending at the last, not slips.)

  Error-free to 2%, broken at 3% -- the 3.1% predicted above.
  spec.md 6.2: a 1% resistor and a 2% C0G capacitor give +/-3%.
  So a clockless AMI receiver lands exactly ON its failure point with
  no margin for temperature or ageing, where the same components give
  biphase +/-13% against a +/-3% need -- four times the margin it needs.

  And look at what it does when it fails: at 3% the constant-0 case
  loses 765 bits.  A mis-rounded gap emits the wrong NUMBER of zeros,
  so the failures are SLIPS.  A clockless receiver would hand back the
  one property that made AMI worth considering (11.1: bit count exactly
  preserved under every impulse tested).  It is not a real option.


OPTION C -- local crystal: does it reintroduce slips?
  spec.md 1 rests on "no rate mismatch anywhere and slips are
  structurally impossible", so this is the question that matters.
  The crystal is a SAMPLING reference; the DPLL recovers bit phase
  from the line, so the bit clock stays locked to the far transmitter.
  If that is right, the decoder emits exactly as many bits as were
  sent however far the two crystals drift apart.

   crystal offset  drift over the clip   bits emitted - sent       BER
             0 ppm               0.0 bits                     0   0.0e+00
           100 ppm               4.8 bits                     0   0.0e+00
           200 ppm               9.6 bits                     0   0.0e+00
          1000 ppm              48.0 bits                     0   0.0e+00
          5000 ppm             240.0 bits                     0   0.0e+00

  The bit count is exact at every offset, including 5000 ppm where
  the two clocks drift by 240 bits across the clip.  The DPLL absorbs
  the drift as phase, not as bits, so spec.md 1 survives: a local
  crystal used as a SAMPLING reference does not cause slips.  Only a
  free-running local clock used as the DATA clock would.


SUMMARY -- what an AMI receiver needs
  option                             reference              verdict
  A  quantise mark-to-mark gaps      one RC, no crystal     needs <3.1%, RC gives 3% -- and fails by slipping
  B  tank flywheeled off the line    resonator per rate     needs Q~100 AND +/-0.25% (9.2): ceramic, not LC
  C  local crystal + DPLL            4.096 MHz, already specified works to 5000 ppm, no slips, every rate an exact divide

  So: yes, the receiver needs a frequency reference, and C is the one
  that works.  What it costs is not a part -- the crystal is already
  on the board -- but the ARCHITECTURAL property spec.md 6.1 opens
  with: "No PLL, no oversampling, no clock on the receive timing path".

  It is NOT a loss of rate-blindness: the receiver already carries a
  per-rate plug (spec.md 2.2, line-receiver.md 8) for the input filter,
  classifier, dead time and carrier detect.  On that axis C is better --
  the per-rate change becomes a counter tap, exact and digital, instead
  of matched RC values, and 4.096 MHz divides every rate exactly.
  Repeaters are unaffected either way: they slice and re-drive and
  never see the frame (spec.md 2.2).

LINE CODES ON THE mfspec FRAME -- structural properties, measured
  Intervals and disparity in BIT periods.  min interval sets the max
  fundamental; max interval sets what clock recovery must coast; the
  disparity swing is the DC wander a transformer has to pass.

  --- payload: random  (line ones density 0.451)
  code                       levels  min int  max int   mean DC  disparity
  Manchester (biphase-L)          2     0.50     1.00    +0.000        1.0
  differential Manchester         2     0.50     1.00    +0.000        1.0
  AMI                             3     1.00    13.00    +0.000        1.0
  unipolar NRZ                    2     1.00    14.00    +0.451    14420.0
  unipolar RZ                     2     0.50    13.50    +0.225     7210.0
  polar NRZ                       2     1.00    14.00    -0.099     3168.5
  NRZI                            2     1.00    14.00    +0.000      186.0
  CMI                             2     0.50     1.50    +0.000        1.5
  Miller                          2     1.00     2.00    +0.002       96.5

  --- payload: constant 0  (line ones density 0.078)
  code                       levels  min int  max int   mean DC  disparity
  Manchester (biphase-L)          2     0.50     1.00    +0.000        1.0
  differential Manchester         2     0.50     1.00    +0.000        1.0
  AMI                             3     1.00    15.00    +0.000        1.0
  unipolar NRZ                    2     1.00    15.00    +0.078     2500.0
  unipolar RZ                     2     0.50    15.50    +0.039     1250.0
  polar NRZ                       2     1.00    15.00    -0.844    26999.5
  NRZI                            2     1.00    16.00    +0.000       30.0
  CMI                             2     0.50     1.50    +0.000        1.5
  Miller                          2     1.00     1.50    +0.016      502.0

  --- payload: constant 1  (line ones density 0.828)
  code                       levels  min int  max int   mean DC  disparity
  Manchester (biphase-L)          2     0.50     1.00    +0.000        1.0
  differential Manchester         2     0.50     1.00    +0.000        1.0
  AMI                             3     1.00     2.00    +0.000        1.0
  unipolar NRZ                    2     1.00    14.00    +0.828    26500.0
  unipolar RZ                     2     0.50     2.50    +0.414    13250.0
  polar NRZ                       2     1.00    14.00    +0.656    21002.0
  NRZI                            2     1.00     3.00    +0.000        6.0
  CMI                             2     0.50     1.50    +0.000        1.5
  Miller                          2     1.00     2.00    +0.000        5.0

  READING IT.  The disparity column decides the candidate set, because
  the pair is transformer coupled (spec.md 5) and a transformer cannot
  pass unbounded DC wander:
    bounded, order 1 bit period : Manchester, differential Manchester,
                                  AMI, CMI  -> viable
    data-dependent, 5-500 bits  : Miller, NRZI              -> not viable
    100s to 10000s of bits      : unipolar NRZ/RZ, polar NRZ -> not viable

  That is the whole answer to "is there anything we skipped".  The two
  skipped codes with a REAL advantage are Miller and NRZI: both halve
  the bandwidth (min interval 1.0 bit against the biphase family's 0.5),
  and Miller also bounds its max interval at 2.0 bits, which would make
  clock recovery nearly as easy as biphase.  Both fail on DC: Miller
  swings 96 bit periods on random payload and 502 on constant 0, NRZI
  186 and 30.  Miller-squared exists precisely to patch this, at the
  cost of encoder state.  Neither is worth simulating as it stands.

  CMI is the interesting near-miss: two levels, DC-free, disparity 1.5,
  max interval 1.5 bits.  It matches differential Manchester on every
  axis and beats it on none -- same 0.5-bit min interval, so the same
  bandwidth and the same reach.  No reason to prefer it.

  Manchester (biphase-L) is structurally identical to differential
  Manchester in this table.  It differs in ONE respect the table cannot
  show: it carries data in the DIRECTION of the mid-cell transition, so
  inverting the pair swaps ones and zeros.  It is not polarity-blind,
  and 12 measured that differential Manchester is -- for free.  That is
  the entire reason to prefer the differential form.

THE FAMILY.  F + S + N channels must be a power of two, so N = 2, 6, 14
  (the 16-bit frame's 2+2N rule gave N = 1, 3, 7; this frame gives more
  channels per bit because signalling is sub-commutated, 2).

   frame   N  per-ch    line      baud  capacity  reach  superframe
      4b   2     16k     64k    0.128M       32k  2.00x        4 fr
      4b   2     32k    128k    0.256M       64k  1.41x        4 fr
      4b   2     64k    256k    0.512M      128k  1.00x        4 fr

      8b   6     16k    128k    0.256M       96k  1.41x        8 fr
      8b   6     32k    256k    0.512M      192k  1.00x        8 fr
      8b   6     64k    512k    1.024M      384k  0.71x        8 fr

     16b  14     16k    256k    0.512M      224k  1.00x       16 fr
     16b  14     32k    512k    1.024M      448k  0.71x       16 fr
     16b  14     64k   1024k    2.048M      896k  0.50x       16 fr

  reach is the sqrt(f) law scaled to the 256 kbps reference, as
  spec.md 2.1 does -- ratios sound, absolutes not measured.

  Read it by ROW for "what does 64 kHz cost me", but read it by
  COLUMN for the choice that actually matters.  At one line rate --
  one reach -- the frame length decides how it is divided:

    256 kbps, 1.00x reach (order 1.7 km):
       4-bit frame @ 64 kHz ->  2 channels x 64 kbps   (DS0 rate)
       8-bit frame @ 32 kHz ->  6 channels x 32 kbps
      16-bit frame @ 16 kHz -> 14 channels x 16 kbps
    512 kbps, 0.71x reach (order 1.2 km):
       8-bit frame @ 64 kHz ->  6 channels x 64 kbps   (DS0 rate)
      16-bit frame @ 32 kHz -> 14 channels x 32 kbps

  So "64 kHz or 32 kHz" is not the question.  Pick the reach the
  plant needs, then pick how to divide it.  64 kbps channels are
  available at ANY reach -- at 2 channels for 1.7 km, 6 for 1.2 km.


WHAT THE PER-CHANNEL RATE COSTS THE AUDIO
  CVSD alone, 2 s clip, one talker:

   per-channel    segSNR  vs 64 kbps
          16k   6.46 dB    -9.06 dB
          32k  10.04 dB    -5.48 dB
          64k  15.52 dB    +0.00 dB

  (spec.md 2.1 on seven talkers: 10.8 dB at 16k, 17.4 at 32k,
  23.3 at 64k, worst talker 9.0 / 14.9 / 19.7.  Specify against the
  worst talker: 16 kbps voice is intelligible but visibly degraded,
  32 kbps is the quality knee, 64 kbps is comfortable.)


REACH IS NOT FREE EITHER -- the same span at three line rates
  Attenuation goes as sqrt(f), so a fixed physical span costs each
  rate a different loss.  Referenced to 12 dB at 256 kbps:

     line    loss  amplitude  hysteresis % of rx
     128k    8.5dB      0.376                 13%
     256k   12.0dB      0.251                 20%
     512k   17.0dB      0.142                 35%

  spec.md 6.1 allows 10% hysteresis, 20% on a single span, so the
  512 kbps column is already outside it on this span -- 8.2 measured
  the cliff exactly there.

  Link on that span, 0.25 s x 4 seeds, random payload, 100 impulses/s.
  Noise floor and impulse rate are FIXED in absolute terms; only the
  rate changes.  Everything is reported in TIME, not in frames: a
  re-acquisition costs a fixed number of FRAMES (7.4), which is four
  times longer in wall-clock at a 16 kHz frame rate than at 64 kHz,
  so a frame-counted outage would flatter the low rates.

     line    amp  breaks/s  ms per break  time down
     128k  0.376        41         13.3 ms      54.7%
     256k  0.251        74          5.0 ms      36.8%
     512k  0.142        87          2.5 ms      21.3%

  The two effects pull OPPOSITE ways, which is not the intuition:
    a lower rate breaks LESS OFTEN -- its signal is larger against a
      fixed impulse amplitude, so fewer rings clear the 1-h threshold
      (10.1), and each ring spans fewer bits
    but each break costs MORE TIME -- re-acquisition is counted in
      frames, and frames are longer
  On these numbers the second effect wins and the lower rate is down
  for more of the time.  So reach does NOT buy impulse robustness for
  free; it buys fewer, longer outages.  Which is preferable is a
  question about the traffic, not about the link: for voice, fewer
  long mutes are worse than more short ones.

  (0.25 s x 4 seeds at one impulse rate.  The ORDERING is the result;
  the magnitudes are thin and should not be quoted as rates.)


THE PCM ARGUMENT -- stronger than it first looks
  A 64 kbps slot is a DS0 rate, so a data card at a 64 kHz frame rate
  is a G.711 pipe.  But rate alone is not enough: PCM needs OCTET
  alignment to know where a sample starts, and data-card.md records
  that the pipe is "not octet-aligned -- the framer establishes bit
  alignment and slot identity, and nothing coarser".

  This frame fixes that for free.  One channel takes one bit per
  frame, and the superframe is 8 frames, so:

    N  superframe  bits/ch/sf  frame kHz  superframe kHz  = PCM 8 kHz?
    2        4 fr           4        16k            4.0k              
    2        4 fr           4        32k            8.0k              
    2        4 fr           4        64k           16.0k              

    6        8 fr           8        16k            2.0k              
    6        8 fr           8        32k            4.0k              
    6        8 fr           8        64k            8.0k           YES

   14       16 fr          16        16k            1.0k              
   14       16 fr          16        32k            2.0k              
   14       16 fr          16        64k            4.0k              

  The elegant case is N = 6 at a 64 kHz frame rate, and it is better
  than a rate coincidence.  THE SUPERFRAME COUNTER IS THE OCTET
  COUNTER.  It has 8 phases; a channel takes one bit per frame; so
  superframe state k IS bit k of that channel's octet.  The same three
  bits that address the signalling demux (the 74xx259) do the byte
  framing, with no extra counter and no extra state:

    superframe state   0  1  2  3  4  5  6  7
    signalling S       A1 A2 A3 P1 A4 A5 A6 P0   -> the 259 address
    channel n octet    b0 b1 b2 b3 b4 b5 b6 b7   -> the byte boundary

  And at a 64 kHz frame rate the superframe rate is 64/8 = 8 kHz,
  the PCM sample rate exactly, so one superframe carries one sample.
  A PCM card is then a shift register clocked by the frame clock and
  transferred at state 7 -- one 74xx164 plus a latch per channel.
  OCTET ALIGNMENT COMES FREE.  The slot becomes a true DS0, not merely
  a 64 kbps pipe -- which is what data-card.md says it is not today.

  No other combination lands on 8 kHz: N = 6 at 32 kHz gives a 4 kHz
  superframe (an octet, but at half the PCM rate), and N = 14 at 64 kHz
  gives 16 bits at 4 kHz -- two octets per superframe, also usable, but
  it is N = 6 at 64 kHz that is one-for-one with a DS0.

  The catch, and it is a serious one: 4.3 measured that the
  superframe phase is admitted wrong for 56 of 64 hook states.  Today
  that misroutes signalling.  If PCM octet alignment is carried on the
  same phase, a wrong phase ROTATES EVERY PCM SAMPLE -- garbage audio
  on a link that is otherwise bit-perfect.  Relying on the superframe
  for octets raises 4.3 from a signalling bug to a payload bug, and
  makes 15 item 3 a precondition rather than a recommendation.

